Q: How to extend subset of products by ones determined by few product groups? A: <p>I think your model is flawed. You should have <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/modeling/relationships/navigations" rel="nofollow noreferrer">navigation properties</a>.</p>
<p>I am not 100% sure if this fits your needs and whether the relations are 1 to n or m to n, but it should probably be something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Product
{
    [Key]
    public string Code { get; set; }
    public string CategoryCode { get; set; }
    public string ProductLineCode { get; set; }

    public string Name { get; set; }
    //... other attributes

    // Navigation properties
    public UserProductCategory ProductCategory { get; set; }
    public UserProductLine ProductLine { get; set; }

     // If product always belongs to 0 or 1 group
    public ProductGroup ProductGroup { get; set; }

     // Otherwise, if product can belong to more than 1 group
    public List&lt;ProductGroup&gt; ProductGroups { get; set; }
}

public class UserProductCategory
{
    [Key]
    public string CategoryCode { get; set; }
    public string Login { get; set; }

    // Navigation property
    public List&lt;Product&gt; Products { get; set; }
}

public class UserProductLine
{
    [Key]
    public string ProductLineCode { get; set; }
    public string Login { get; set; }

    // Navigation property
    public List&lt;Product&gt; Products { get; set; }
}

public class ProductGroup
{
    [Key]
    public string GroupCode { get; set; }
    public List&lt;Product&gt; Products { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you can perform a query like this</p>
<pre><code>var products = dbContext.Products
    .Where(p =&gt; (p.ProductCategory.Login = login &amp;&amp;
                p.ProductLine.Login = login) ||
                p.ProductGroups.Any(g =&gt; lst.Contains(g.GroupCode));
</code></pre>
<p>Note that navigation properties don't need to be populated for the query to work. Here they are only needed to tell EF-Core how the entities are related and allow it to generate the correct SQL.</p>
<p>I don't know enough about your data, but there might be additional 1 to n relations, e.g. between <code>Product</code> and <code>UserProductCategory</code> or <code>Product</code> and <code>UserProductLine</code>. And <code>Login</code> could be a separate entity with relations to product line and product category to create a fully normalized schema.</p>
<p>See also:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/54255193/880990">Necessity of navigation properties in EF Core models</a>.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.learnentityframeworkcore.com/relationships" rel="nofollow noreferrer">EF Core Relationships</a>.</li>
</ul>
